  data:  # 6e4e
    kind: work_item  # 6e4e
    title: "Author stdlib_module_completeness for the python working set: 1 module enumerated across 14 catalogues, so a call the catalogue cannot classify can never be confirmed over"  # 6e4e
    status: todo_hard  # 6e4e
    priority: 2  # 6e4e
    parent:  # 6e4e
    tags: []  # 6e4e
    isbefore: []  # 6e4e
    duplicate_of: []  # 6e4e
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 6e4e
    pr_ref:  # 6e4e
    description: "THE FOLLOW-UP THAT WIDENS WHERE `confirmed` IS REACHABLE. INV-buzab closed the\ngate so that a clean verdict needs either a CLASSIFIED call or an ENUMERATED\nmodule.\n\nSCOPE IS SMALLER THAN IT FIRST LOOKED, and the correction matters. An\nintermediate draft of that fix answered per-MODULE, which would have made this\nitem urgent and enormous. The shipped rule answers per-CALL-SITE: a call the\ncatalogue classifies is already examined, so completeness entries are needed\nONLY for the calls it cannot classify. Programs whose external calls are all\ncatalogued still confirm today — measured, a fixture doing `json.dump(obj, fh)`\ninside `open(...)` confirms `no net_send` and correctly reports `violated` for\n`fs_write`. Exactly one module is enumerated across all 14 shipped\ncatalogues (python `math`), so what remains unreachable is confirmation over a\nprogram that calls a stdlib name the catalogue does not carry a row for.\n\nThat is the honest starting state, not a regression: before the fix the gate was\nconfirming over 283 python stdlib modules it had never examined, and a Go\nprogram with no filesystem I/O at all was already returning inconclusive naming\n`fmt`, `sort` and `strings`. Nothing got worse for any repo measured (six repos,\nsix languages, zero verdict changes). But a tool whose all-clear is unreachable\nin practice tells a user very little, and the plan's own guard against the\nblanket-downgrade failure mode applies here rather than to the gate change.\n\nWHAT AUTHORING AN ENTRY MEANS, so this is not mistaken for bookkeeping. An entry\nin `stdlib_module_completeness` is the claim \"I audited this module and every\nI/O primitive it exposes is enumerated in the boundary sections above, so an\nunmatched call into it is provably not I/O.\" It is now the single grant of\nconfirmability: adding one converts \"I cannot confirm this\" into \"I confirm\nthis\" for every repo that touches the module, and a wrong entry is a false\nall-clear on a security claim. `scripts/refresh-stdlib-modules` already rules\nout generating them — \"closed-world claims demand per-module human audit, not a\nscript that scrapes interpreter state\" — and that stays true.\n\n*** THE STANDING HAZARD, NAMED UP FRONT: this replaces a mechanical falsehood\nwith a human one that carries a date and an author's authority, which reads MORE\ntrustworthy and is therefore more dangerous when wrong. This project has shipped\nover-confident completeness declarations before — `rust.yaml` and `erlang.yaml`\ndeclare `status: complete` with zero stdlib_modules, and python.yaml's own\nprovenance notes admit `stdlib_other` was never populated. Whatever procedure\nthis item lands must make a wrong entry findable, not just fast to write. ***\n\nTHE MEASURED BACKLOG, from the six-repo cohort (httpx, full-stack-fastapi-\ntemplate, poetry, hypergumbo, pretix, django) via the in-repo branch-attribution\ninstrument: 172 distinct python stdlib module STRINGS were reaching the gate on\nname recognition alone; 105 once reduced to top-level module names. None carried\na completeness flag. That is the working set — not the whole 300-entry stdlib —\nand it is what would have to be audited to restore confirmability on realistic\npython repos.\n\nThe split is uneven and the cheap half is genuinely cheap. A large share are\npure-computation modules where \"no I/O primitives\" settles in minutes\n(`re`, `typing`, `itertools`, `functools`, `collections`, `bisect`, `heapq`,\n`operator`, `string`, `textwrap`, `dataclasses`, `enum`, `keyword`, `graphlib`,\n`statistics`, `decimal`). The expensive half is not declaration work at all, it\nis CATALOGUE work: modules that are I/O-capable with ZERO rows today, where an\nhonest `complete` requires ADDING primitive rows first —\n`ssl`, `ctypes`, `glob`, `zipfile`, `tarfile`, `gzip`, `lzma`, `bz2`, `netrc`,\n`plistlib`, `configparser`, `logging`, `wsgiref`, `site`, `sysconfig`,\n`importlib.metadata`, `select`, `signal`, `fcntl`, `termios`, `readline`,\n`getpass`, `secrets`, `uuid`, `winreg`, `msvcrt`, `resource`, `pwd`, `grp`.\nThree of those (`ssl`, `ctypes`, and `os`'s unenumerated tail) are the ones that\nproduced live false confirms in INV-buzab's repro, so they are also recall wins\nindependent of the gate.\n\nDO NOT START THIS BY DECLARING MODULES COMPLETE. Sequence it:\n 1. Decide and write down what evidence an entry requires and how a wrong one\n    gets caught. A validator that cross-checks a `complete` module against the\n    rows actually present would be the minimum; today nothing does.\n 2. Add the missing primitive ROWS for the I/O-capable modules above. That is\n    ordinary recall work and it stands on its own merits.\n 3. Only then declare completeness, in small batches, each with its `retrieved:`\n    date and a re-measurement of the confirmable population.\n\nThe 13 non-python catalogues have zero completeness entries and the same\nquestion. They are not in scope here beyond being named: this item is python,\nbecause python is the language the six-repo measurement covers and the one the\ntrial is most likely to run on.\n\n\nMATCHING IS EXACT, so declare every module you audited, submodules included. A\nprefix rule was drafted and refuted: `urllib` is a namespace package whose\nsubmodules have unrelated I/O surfaces, Go's `/` is not containment (a\ndeclaration for `os` would have vouched for `os/exec`), and Rust's `::` was not\nin the separator list at all.\n\nAND THE OVERLAY CHANNEL IS CLOSED TO THIS FIELD. A project-local overlay may\nsupply ROWS (which add detection — the safe direction) but may NOT declare\ncompleteness; it raises IoPrimitiveOverlayError. So this authoring work cannot\nbe delegated to users, which is the right call and also means the backlog below\nis entirely maintainer work."  # 6e4e
